# Release prep for v2.0.0-alpha.6. GATE — run `sbt test` before tagging; CI runs it again.
#
#   sed -n "/^--- MESSAGE ---$/,\$p" draco-git-record/git-record-2026-08-17-1730 | sed '1d' > /tmp/msg
#   git add -A && git commit -F /tmp/msg && git push
#
# THEN, only after the push succeeds:
#   git tag -a v2.0.0-alpha.6 -m "v2.0.0-alpha.6"
#   git push origin v2.0.0-alpha.6
#
# The tag push starts .github/workflows/release.yml, which now checks the tag against
# build.sbt and the notes file before building, runs the suite, assembles the jar, and
# publishes with the written notes as the release body. No `gh release edit` step is
# needed any more. Watch it:
#   gh run watch $(gh run list --workflow=release.yml --limit 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId')

--- MESSAGE ---
Release v2.0.0-alpha.6

Cuts the [Unreleased] block to a version block dated today, opens a fresh empty one, and
bumps the build to 2.0.0-alpha.6. Release notes in
draco-git-record/release-notes-v2.0.0-alpha.6.md, following the alpha.5 convention.

Two and a half months and 37 commits since alpha.5, which is far too long a gap and is the
reason this release exists at all: nothing between 2026-06-03 and today is visible to
anyone outside the tree, including the entire DRAKE language. The CHANGELOG trigger landed
in the previous commit so that the next gap is a commit wide rather than a quarter.

The notes lead with what changed rather than with a feature list: a definition surface of
draco's own, values as normative trees rather than target source text, loading as an
explicit language-neutral path, self-validation through the shipped rule engine, role as
aspect-presence, and the first type constructor to leave the host's vocabulary.

They also carry a Known limitations section, which an alpha should. Scala is the only
realized target; `Drake.parse` is a measurement tool rather than an authoring path (#61);
the surface CLI has no shell wrapper; and the metamodel is NOT frozen — `TypeName` and
`derivation` are expected to change shape under #51 before beta. Saying that plainly is
what makes the alpha honest and what makes the eventual beta mean something.

THE RELEASE WORKFLOW, repaired in the same commit because it had not run since alpha.5 and
would probably have failed.

It installed sbt by adding the scala-sbt debian repo and calling `apt-key add`, which is
deprecated and removed on current runner images, after a keyserver lookup at build time —
two external dependencies and one dead command, on a `runs-on: ubuntu-latest` that has
moved underneath it in the intervening two and a half months. Replaced with the official
`sbt/setup-sbt` action.

Then two integrity checks, which are the part worth keeping. A release is three artifacts
that must agree — the tag, the version the build stamps into the jar, and the written
notes — and nothing checked that they did. A tag pushed against a stale build.sbt would
have published a jar named for the previous version, and that is not hypothetical: bumping
build.sbt is a separate manual step from tagging, and it is exactly the step a person
forgets. Both checks now run before anything expensive, and fail in seconds. Verified
locally against this commit using the same commands the workflow runs.

The written notes are now the release body via `body_path`, with GitHub's generated commit
list appended beneath, so the manual `gh release edit --notes-file` step that alpha.5
needed is gone. `fail_on_unmatched_files` means a missing jar fails the run rather than
publishing an empty release.

The same definitions-move-as-a-trio discipline the corpus has, applied to a release.
